     1	# arXiv:2306.08594 — directed resolving sets in DAGs
     2	
     3	> **Build status:** the generator and all local gates G1–G9(c) pass. The required external hardening verdict is incomplete: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded (total limit)” on every bare, hinted, and placebo redraw. Those errors are preserved in the script-owned transcripts and are not counted as oracle failures.
     4	
     5	| Profile field | Value |
     6	|---|---|
     7	| Track | **A** — structural hardness |
     8	| Native domain | combinatorics |
     9	| Object regime | finite discrete |
    10	| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
    11	| Certificate | integer tuple: a normal-form directed resolving set |
    12	| Intuition | reduction recognition: all unresolved information is concentrated in same-layer sink pairs |
    13	| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
    14	| Reduction | DAG-section NP-completeness proof, Hitting Set → Directed Metric Dimension |
    15	
    16	## What the family is
    17	
    18	Schmitz and Wanke’s [*The directed metric dimension of directed co-graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08594) defines a directed resolving set using distances **from** selected vertices. Its final section proves that Directed Metric Dimension is NP-complete even for directed acyclic graphs by transforming a Hitting Set family into a layered DAG.
    19	
    20	This module uses that construction verbatim. It first samples one value from each choice group, then creates balanced NAE-style set pairs that the sample hits. For every set `F_j`, the DAG contains vertices `P_j,Q_j`. The source vertices `a,b,c` distinguish all vertex pairs except `P_j,Q_j`; a selected ground vertex separates that pair exactly when it lies in `F_j`. Thus `a,b,c` plus the planted transversal is known before the DAG exists. `verify` reconstructs every exact directed-distance signature and compares them; it never reads `inst["answer"]`.
    21	
    22	## Why Track A, and the easy regime avoided
    23	
    24	Section 3, Theorem 2 gives the relevant worst-case result: the reduction preserves a Hitting Set of size `k` as a resolving set of size `k+3`. The shipping preset has 36 growing choice groups, eight values per group, 252 balanced NAE rows, 540 Hitting Set members, and a normal-form space of `8^36`. Plants and decoys have exactly equal incidence frequency because every row is paired with its complement and every group appears once per regular round.
    25	
    26	The strongest implemented baseline is q-ary DPLL with MRV and NAE unit propagation. Its worst case is `O(q^groups)`. At shipping size it exhausted 20,000 nodes in **12.714 s** on the final G5 run without solving; across eight G6 seeds it exhausted 160,000 nodes in **72.013 s** and solved 0/8. Incidence outlier, one-pass greedy, and 64 min-conflicts restarts of 720 steps each also solved 0/8.
    27	
    28	The paper’s main positive result is deliberately not used: Section 2, Theorem 1 computes a minimum resolving set for a strongly connected directed co-graph in linear time. Such a family would fail Track A. The number of choice groups grows through the named ladder to 36; beyond `hard`, `escalate` enlarges each group’s value haystack while keeping the 39-atom answer and the at-most-300-check route fixed.
    29	
    30	The Track-A qualification matters: the paper proves worst-case NP-completeness, not average-case hardness for this planted distribution. The measured attacks support the distributional claim but do not prove it; see Caveats.
    31	
    32	## Worked demo
    33	
    34	This is the complete rendering of `make_instance(n=2, groups=3, rounds=3, dpll_node_budget=500, seed=0)`:
    35	
    36	```text
    37	DIRECTED RESOLVING SET IN A RULE-DEFINED DAG
    38	
    39	For vertices u,v in a directed graph, d(u,v) is the number of arcs in a shortest directed path from u to v. If no such path exists, d(u,v) is undefined. An undefined distance is not equal to, and cannot be compared with, any distance. A vertex w resolves distinct u,v when w is u or v, or when both d(w,u) and d(w,v) are defined and unequal. A set R is resolving when every pair of graph vertices is resolved by some w in R.
    40	
    41	There are 6 integer-labelled ground vertices, partitioned into 3 displayed choice groups G_0,...,G_2, each of size 2. Group order and the order inside a group are significant only for the required answer format:
    42	G_0: 5 3
    43	G_1: 2 1
    44	G_2: 6 4
    45	
    46	Define a family F_0,...,F_8 of 9 subsets of the ground vertices. For 0 <= i < 3, F_i=G_i. Each row t below has three group indices i,j,k and three displayed half-groups A,B,C. It defines F_(g+2t)=A union B union C and F_(g+2t+1)=(G_i\A) union (G_j\B) union (G_k\C). Complements are within the named group; no other ground vertex belongs to either set.
    47	
    48	t | i:A | j:B | k:C
    49	0 | 2:6 | 0:3 | 1:2
    50	1 | 2:6 | 1:1 | 0:5
    51	2 | 1:1 | 0:3 | 2:6
    52	
    53	The directed graph has source vertices a,b,c; every integer ground vertex; and two vertices P_j,Q_j for each set F_j. Its arcs are exactly these:
    54	1. a has an arc to every ground vertex.
    55	2. In the ground order printed below, b has an arc to the first vertex and each ground vertex has an arc to the next one.
    56	3. c has arcs to P_0 and Q_0.
    57	4. P_j has an arc to Q_j for every j.
    58	5. For every j before the last layer, each of P_j,Q_j has arcs to both P_(j+1),Q_(j+1).
    59	6. Every ground vertex x has an arc to every P_j.
    60	7. A ground vertex x has an arc to Q_j exactly when x is not in F_j.
    61	There are no other arcs. All arcs point forward in the displayed construction, so this graph is acyclic.
    62	Ground order: 3 5 6 4 1 2
    63	
    64	Find a resolving set in the required normal form R=["a","b","c",r_0,...,r_2]. For every i, r_i must be exactly one integer label from G_i. The prefix and group order are mandatory, repetitions are forbidden, and the list therefore has exactly 6 entries. Other resolving sets are outside this certificate language.
    65	
    66	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one JSON list. Strings must be quoted and integers must be decimal.
    67	<answer>["a","b","c",1,2,3]</answer> is a format example only; it is not the answer to this instance.
    68	Output nothing else inside the tags.
    69	```
    70	
    71	The planted answer is `<answer>["a","b","c",3,1,4]</answer>`, and `verify` returns `(True, "ok")`. Replacing the last entry by `6` returns `(False, "unresolved layer pair P_8,Q_8: the selected vertices miss F_8")`. A person can solve this demo by checking only `2^3=8` normal-form candidates.
    72	
    73	## Difficulty presets
    74	
    75	| Preset | Values/group | Groups | Rounds | Ground vertices | Set layers | DAG vertices | Answer atoms | Status |
    76	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    77	| demo | 2 | 3 | 3 | 6 | 9 | 27 | 6 | hand example |
    78	| easy | 4 | 18 | 9 | 72 | 126 | 327 | 21 | oracle not reached: key limit |
    79	| medium | 6 | 24 | 12 | 144 | 216 | 579 | 27 | locally available |
    80	| hard | 8 | 36 | 21 | 288 | 540 | 1,371 | 39 | provisional shipping preset |
    81	
    82	No current preset was rejected by a local gate. An earlier candidate for `hard` (30 groups, 18 rounds) was discarded after uncapped DPLL found a witness in 13,729 nodes and about 3.8 seconds; the former 1,000-node panel had hidden that break. `hard` remains provisional because STEP 4 did not obtain a model response.
    83	
    84	## Gate results
    85	
    86	| Gate | Result | Measurement |
    87	|---|---|---|
    88	| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted certificates verify; every answer JSON-round-trips |
    89	| G2 | pass | required five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons; an additional same-group substitution also rejected |
    90	| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered through prose and a Markdown fence |
    91	| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses from `8^36` candidates |
    92	| G5 | pass | shipping observed density 0/200,000; DPLL 20,000 nodes, 12.714 s, unsolved; demo has exactly 4/8 answers |
    93	| G6 | pass | frequency, greedy, 64-restart min-conflicts, and 20,000-node exact DPLL all 0/8; DPLL aggregate 160,000 nodes / 72.013 s |
    94	| G7 | pass | all named work sizes grow; doubling `n` to 16 builds 1,803 vertices and verifies |
    95	| G8 | pass | 60/60 relabelling invariance checks and 60/60 carried witnesses; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
    96	| G9(c) | pass | 142 chars, 36 estimated tokens, 39 atoms, 288 intended operations |
    97	
    98	## Oracle loop
    99	
   100	The bare transcript was written only by `scripts/harden.py`. These are API errors, not failed solving attempts, so there is no hardness verdict.
   101	
   102	| Preset | Model | Seed | Result | Why |
   103	|---|---|---:|---|---|
   104	| easy | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | 275016123 | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 total key limit |
   105	| easy | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | 1334728332 | error | redraw; same HTTP 403 |
   106	| easy | Claude Sonnet 5 | 1172592643 | error | redraw; same HTTP 403 |
   107	| easy | Claude Sonnet 5 | 567362756 | error | redraw; same HTTP 403; harness stopped |
   108	
   109	## G9 arms
   110	
   111	| Arm | Solved / attempts | Script calls | Status |
   112	|---|---:|---:|---|
   113	| bare | 0 / 0 | 4 | all HTTP 403; unavailable |
   114	| structural hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | all HTTP 403; unavailable |
   115	| placebo hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | all HTTP 403; unavailable |
   116	
   117	The hinted-minus-placebo diagnostic is unavailable; the numeric `0.0` in `selftest_report.json` is only the zero-attempt sentinel and supports no conclusion about the stated intuition. The structural hint names only the same-layer signature invariant. The size/effort arm passes at 142 characters, 39 atoms, and 288 operations.
   118	
   119	## Use
   120	
   121	```python
   122	from gen_2306_08594 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, parse_answer, verify
   123	
   124	inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
   125	assert verify(inst, inst["answer"]) == (True, "ok")
   126	raw = "Reasoning omitted. <answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>"
   127	assert parse_answer(raw) == inst["answer"]
   128	```
   129	
   130	From the repository root, after obtaining a valid hardening verdict:
   131	
   132	```bash
   133	bash scripts/emit.sh 2306.08594 20 hard
   134	```
   135	
   136	## Caveats
   137	
   138	Theorem 2 proves worst-case NP-completeness for DAGs; it does **not** prove this balanced planted distribution hard. The 0/200,000 result is an observed rate under the precise prior “uniformly choose one displayed value per group”; it is not a confidence proof that every informed strategy has probability below `1e-6`. The exact baseline is node-capped, so it shows that this particular search prefix is costly, not that uncapped DPLL never finishes.
   139	
   140	No external SAT/SMT/ILP package, LP relaxation, SDP, or planted-CSP spectral/tensor recovery was available or run. A successful one would invalidate Track A and require redesign or an honest Track-B relabelling. The canonical key is a strong multiset of complementary-clause intersection statistics, not a complete hypergraph-isomorphism canonizer. Finally, the mandatory multi-vendor oracle evidence is missing because of the external key limit; this directory is reproducible and locally verified but must not be submitted as hardened until all three arms are rerun successfully.

