codex
Implemented the verified Track A generator using the paper’s Theorem 2 Hitting Set → DAG reduction. Theorem 1’s linear-time directed co-graph regime was correctly excluded.

Local results:

- All G1–G9(c) gates pass.
- Structure-aware guesses: 0/200,000 in a \(10^{36}\) space.
- Four attacks: 0/8 each.
- DPLL baseline: 20,000 nodes and 9.127 seconds.
- Answer: 152 characters, 39 atoms.
- Exact symbolic verification cross-checked against explicit graph BFS.

I also corrected a subtle reduction issue by adding an unused first ground vertex, preventing source `b` from accidentally resolving a missed set-layer pair.

Files:

- [gen_2306_08594.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2306.08594/gen_2306_08594.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2306.08594/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2306.08594/README.md)
- [bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2306.08594/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2306.08594/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2306.08594/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The only incomplete requirement is external oracle hardening: every OpenRouter call returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded.” Those script-generated errors are preserved, and the README marks the hard preset provisional. Once quota is restored, rerun:

```bash
cd /home/gson/gvrepo/results/2306.08594
python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_2306_08594.py
```
